Programming in JavaScript<P>
<HR>
<H1>10  Menus and the icon bar</H1>
<HR>
In this chapter you will turn Doodle into a proper desktop application: it will have a menu, an icon on the
icon bar, an Info box and a colour menu, and it will keep running when its window is closed, as Edit and
Draw do.<P>

<A NAME=S1></A>
<H2>Menus</H2>
Every RISC OS application has a menu, opened by clicking Menu over its window. In JavaScript a menu is made
with <CODE>new Menu(...)</CODE>, which is given the menu's title and a list of <I>items</I>:<P>
<PRE>
const m = new Menu('Doodle2', [
  { text: 'Hello', action: () =&gt; print('Hello') },
  { text: 'Quit', action: () =&gt; task.quit() },
]);
</PRE>
Each item is an object (chapter 5). Its <CODE>text</CODE> is what the menu shows, and its
<CODE>action</CODE> is a function that is called when you choose the item. Here the actions are arrow
functions, as in chapter 4: nothing happens when the menu is made, only when an item is chosen.<P>
The word <CODE>new</CODE> in front of <CODE>Menu</CODE> means "make a new one of these". You'll see
<CODE>new</CODE> in front of a few other names in JavaScript, such as <CODE>new Date()</CODE> in
chapter 9.<P>
To show the menu, call <CODE>wimp.menus.openAt</CODE> when the user clicks Menu over the window. It needs the
menu, the click event (so it knows where the pointer is) and the task that owns the menu:<P>
<PRE>
w.on('click', (ev) =&gt; {
  if (ev.button === 'menu') {
    wimp.menus.openAt(m, ev, { task });
  }
  return true;
});
</PRE>
<CODE>{ task }</CODE> is a short way of writing <CODE>{ task: task }</CODE>: an object with a property called
<CODE>task</CODE> whose value is the variable <CODE>task</CODE>.<P>

<H3>More about menu items</H3>
Items can have other properties besides <CODE>text</CODE> and <CODE>action</CODE>:<P>
<DL>
<DT><CODE>ticked: true</CODE>
<DD>shows a tick beside the item, for an option that is on.<P>
<DT><CODE>shaded: true</CODE>
<DD>greys the item out, so it can't be chosen - for example Clear when there's nothing to clear.<P>
<DT><CODE>dotted: true</CODE>
<DD>draws a dotted line under the item, to divide the menu into groups.<P>
<DT><CODE>submenu: ...</CODE>
<DD>gives the item an arrow, and something that opens when you move the pointer over the arrow: another
menu, or a dialogue box such as an Info box or a Save box.<P>
</DL>

<A NAME=S2></A>
<H2>Doodle2</H2>
Here is Doodle from chapter 8 with a menu and an icon bar icon added. It looks long, but most of it is
Doodle exactly as it was; the new parts are explained below.<P>
<PRE>
// Doodle2 - Doodle with a menu and an icon on the icon bar
let colour = 7;       // the Wimp colour we are using (7 is black)
const lines = [];     // everything drawn so far
let drawing = null;   // the line being drawn, while Select is held

const w = task.createWindow({
  title: 'Doodle2',
  x: 160, y: 100, w: 480, h: 360,
  extent: { w: 480, h: 360 },
  flags: { title: true, close: true, back: true, moveable: true },
  colours: { workBg: 0 },
  workButton: 'click',
});

w.useCanvas((g) =&gt; {
  g.lineWidth = 4;
  g.lineCap = 'round';
  g.lineJoin = 'round';
  for (const line of lines) {
    g.strokeStyle = line.colour;
    g.beginPath();
    g.moveTo(line.points[0].x, line.points[0].y);
    for (const p of line.points) {
      g.lineTo(p.x, p.y);
    }
    g.stroke();
  }
});

// the Info box, made once
const info = infoBox(task, {
  name: 'Doodle2', purpose: 'Draws doodles',
  author: 'A. Programmer', version: '1.00',
});

// the program's menu: made afresh each time it is opened
function doodleMenu() {
  return new Menu('Doodle2', [
    { text: 'Info', submenu: info },
    { text: 'Colour',
      submenu: colourMenu('Colour', colour, (n) =&gt; { colour = n; }) },
    { text: 'Clear', shaded: lines.length === 0, dotted: true,
      action: () =&gt; {
        lines.length = 0;
        w.invalidate();
      } },
    { text: 'Quit', action: () =&gt; task.quit() },
  ]);
}

w.on('click', (ev) =&gt; {
  if (ev.button === 'select') {
    // start a new line where the mouse is
    drawing = { colour: wimpColour(colour), points: [] };
    drawing.points.push({ x: ev.x, y: ev.y });
    lines.push(drawing);
    w.invalidate();
  } else if (ev.button === 'menu') {
    wimp.menus.openAt(doodleMenu(), ev, { task });
  }
  return true;
});

w.on('pointermove', (ev) =&gt; {
  if (drawing &amp;&amp; ev.buttons === 1) {
    drawing.points.push({ x: ev.x, y: ev.y });
    w.invalidate();
  } else {
    drawing = null;
  }
});

// an icon on the icon bar: Select opens the window, Menu the menu
task.addIconbarIcon({
  sprite: 'file_aff',
  onClick: () =&gt; w.open({ behind: 'top' }),
  menu: () =&gt; doodleMenu(),
});

// quit when the desktop is shut down
task.onMessage('Quit', () =&gt; task.quit());

w.open();
</PRE>
Run it, draw something, and click Menu over the window:<P>
<P><CENTER><IMG SRC="PICS/Doodle2Menu" WIDTH=614 HEIGHT=461></CENTER>
The menu has Info, Colour, Clear and Quit. Move the pointer over the arrow beside Colour to choose a colour
for the next line, and try Clear and Quit. Notice the new icon at the right of the icon bar, in the
picture above: that is Doodle2's too. Here is the window again after a little more drawing:<P>
