<A NAME=S3></A>
<H2>Making the menu</H2>
The menu is made by a function, <CODE>doodleMenu</CODE>, which is called each time the menu is opened. Why
not make it once, at the start, as in the example above? Because some of its items change: Clear should be
shaded only while the drawing is empty, and the tick in the colour menu should be beside the colour in use
<I>now</I>. Making the menu afresh each time means it always shows things as they are:<P>
<PRE>
// the program's menu: made afresh each time it is opened
function doodleMenu() {
  return new Menu('Doodle2', [
    { text: 'Info', submenu: info },
    { text: 'Colour',
      submenu: colourMenu('Colour', colour, (n) =&gt; { colour = n; }) },
    { text: 'Clear', shaded: lines.length === 0, dotted: true,
      action: () =&gt; {
        lines.length = 0;
        w.invalidate();
      } },
    { text: 'Quit', action: () =&gt; task.quit() },
  ]);
}
</PRE>
<CODE>shaded: lines.length === 0</CODE> is worked out when the menu is made: <CODE>true</CODE> if there are no
lines. And <CODE>lines.length = 0</CODE>, in Clear's action, empties the array. (<CODE>lines</CODE> is a
<CODE>const</CODE>, so it can't be given a new, empty array, but the array it names can still be
changed.)<P>

<H3>The colour menu</H3>
RISC OS has a standard menu for choosing one of the sixteen desktop colours, and <CODE>colourMenu</CODE> makes
it:<P>
<PRE>
colourMenu('Colour', colour, (n) =&gt; { colour = n; })
</PRE>
It is given the menu's title, the colour to tick, and a function to call with the number of the colour
chosen. The number is a Wimp colour, the same numbers as <CODE>wimpColour</CODE> uses in chapter 7, so
Doodle2 keeps the colour as a number and turns it into a colour for drawing when a line is started:
<CODE>wimpColour(colour)</CODE>.<P>

<H3>The Info box</H3>
Every application's menu starts with Info, leading to a box that says what the program is, who wrote it
and which version it is. <CODE>infoBox</CODE> makes one:<P>
<PRE>
const info = infoBox(task, {
  name: 'Doodle2', purpose: 'Draws doodles',
  author: 'A. Programmer', version: '1.00',
});
</PRE>
It is made once, at the start, and used as the Info item's submenu, so it appears when you move the pointer
over Info's arrow. Put your own name in it.<P>

<A NAME=S4></A>
<H2>An icon on the icon bar</H2>
The icons on the right of the icon bar belong to applications that are running. Clicking Select on one
usually opens a window, and Menu gives the application's menu - even when none of its windows is open.
<CODE>task.addIconbarIcon</CODE> puts one there:<P>
<PRE>
// an icon on the icon bar: Select opens the window, Menu the menu
task.addIconbarIcon({
  sprite: 'file_aff',
  onClick: () =&gt; w.open({ behind: 'top' }),
  menu: () =&gt; doodleMenu(),
});
</PRE>
<DL>
<DT><CODE>sprite</CODE>
<DD>the name of the picture to show. Here it is <CODE>file_aff</CODE>, the Filer's picture for a Draw file,
which suits a drawing program. Any sprite the desktop knows will do: <CODE>application</CODE> is a plain
application icon. Chapter 13 shows how a program can have a picture of its own.<P>
<DT><CODE>onClick</CODE>
<DD>a function called when you click Select (or Adjust) on the icon. Doodle2 opens its window, in front of
the others.<P>
<DT><CODE>menu</CODE>
<DD>the menu to open when you click Menu on the icon: a menu, or a function that makes one, as here. The
desktop opens icon bar menus just above the icon bar, as RISC OS always does.<P>
</DL>
You can also give an icon some <CODE>text</CODE> to show underneath it, as the disc drives have.<P>

<A NAME=S5></A>
<H2>Keeping going without a window</H2>
There is one important difference from Doodle: Doodle2 doesn't have the line<P>
<PRE>
w.on('close', () =&gt; task.quit());
</PRE>
So when you close its window, the window closes but the program carries on: its icon is still on the icon
bar, and clicking it opens the window again, with your drawing still in it. That is how RISC OS
applications normally behave. They stop only when you choose Quit from their menu.<P>
A program keeps running as long as it has something that could make more of it happen: a window, an icon
bar icon, or a timer. A program with none of these has nothing left to do, so it finishes.<P>
You can see Doodle2 in the Task Manager's window (click Select on the Acorn icon at the right of the icon
bar) with the other tasks. Choosing Quit from Doodle2's menu calls <CODE>task.quit()</CODE>, which closes
its windows, removes its icon and ends the task.<P>

<H3>The Quit message</H3>
Programs can also be told to stop from outside: by the Task Manager's menu, or when you shut the desktop
down. Then RISC OS sends each program a <I>message</I> called Quit. A program asks to hear about a message
with <CODE>task.onMessage</CODE>:<P>
<PRE>
// quit when the desktop is shut down
task.onMessage('Quit', () =&gt; task.quit());
</PRE>
A real RISC OS program must stop when it gets a Quit message. Here the desktop stops the program anyway,
but the Quit message is the place to do any tidying up first - saving work that hasn't been saved, for
example.<P>

<A NAME=S6></A>
<H2>Summary</H2>
<UL>
<LI><CODE>new Menu(title, items)</CODE> makes a menu. Each item is an object with <CODE>text</CODE> and an
<CODE>action</CODE> function, and may have <CODE>ticked</CODE>, <CODE>shaded</CODE>, <CODE>dotted</CODE> and
<CODE>submenu</CODE>.<P>
<LI>Open a menu when Menu is clicked with <CODE>wimp.menus.openAt(menu, ev, { task })</CODE>. Make the menu
in a function if its items change.<P>
<LI><CODE>colourMenu(title, colour, pick)</CODE> is the standard colour menu; <CODE>infoBox(task, {...})</CODE>
makes an Info box to use as a submenu.<P>
<LI><CODE>task.addIconbarIcon({ sprite, onClick, menu })</CODE> puts an icon on the icon bar.<P>
<LI>A program keeps running while it has a window, an icon bar icon or a timer. <CODE>task.quit()</CODE>
stops it.<P>
<LI><CODE>task.onMessage('Quit', ...)</CODE> is called when the desktop tells the program to stop.<P>
</UL>

<A NAME=S7></A>
<H2>Exercises</H2>
<DL>
<DT><DD>1  Add a <I>Thick lines</I> item to Doodle2's menu, ticked when it is on, that makes new lines twice
as thick. (Each line will need to remember its own width, as it remembers its colour.)<P>
<DT><DD>2  Give Doodle2's icon bar icon the text <CODE>Doodle</CODE> underneath it.<P>
<DT><DD>3  Add an <I>Undo</I> item that removes the last line drawn. Shade it when there is nothing to
undo. (An array's <CODE>pop</CODE> method removes its last item: <CODE>lines.pop()</CODE>.)<P>
<DT><DD>4  Clicking Adjust in Doodle changed the colour. Put that back into Doodle2, so Adjust moves on to the
next Wimp colour, from 0 to 15 and round again.<P>
</DL>

<A NAME=S8></A>
<H2>Answers</H2>
<DL>
<DT><DD>1  Keep a variable, <CODE>let thick = false;</CODE>, and add the item:
<PRE>
{ text: 'Thick lines', ticked: thick,
  action: () =&gt; { thick = !thick; } },
</PRE>
When a line is started, store its width with it:
<PRE>
    let width = 4;
    if (thick) {
      width = 8;
    }
    drawing = {
      colour: wimpColour(colour), width: width, points: [],
    };
</PRE>
and in the redraw function set <CODE>g.lineWidth = line.width;</CODE> inside the loop, before drawing each
line.<P>
<DT><DD>2  Add <CODE>text: 'Doodle',</CODE> to the object given to <CODE>task.addIconbarIcon</CODE>.<P>
<DT><DD>3  <PRE>
{ text: 'Undo', shaded: lines.length === 0,
  action: () =&gt; {
    lines.pop();
    w.invalidate();
  } },
</PRE>
<DT><DD>4  Add a branch to the click handler:
<PRE>
} else if (ev.button === 'adjust') {
  colour = (colour + 1) % 16;
}
</PRE>
</DL>

